Practice 7 grammar (EBNF): member functions, member calls, the mutation
rule, read()/print() and a call in the middle of a chain, on top of the
Practice 6 language (structs, functions, field access and calls).

Notation: | alternatives, [ x ] optional, { x } zero or more,
"quoted" terminals are token kinds the lexer emits verbatim,
unquoted names are non-terminals with a rule below.

program     ::= { struct } { fn } { statement } exit_stmt
struct      ::= "struct" ident NL "{" NL field_decl { field_decl } { fn } "}" NL
field_decl  ::= type [ "mut" ] ident NL
fn          ::= "fn" ident ":=" "(" [ type ident { "," type ident } ] ")"
                "->" type NL block
statement   ::= decl | assign | if | while | print
decl        ::= type [ "mut" ] ident "{" init_value { "," init_value } "}"
init_value  ::= expr | "{" init_value { "," init_value } "}"
type        ::= "i32" | "i64" | "bool" | ident
assign      ::= ident { "." ident } ":=" expr
if          ::= "if" expr NL block [ "else" NL block ]
while       ::= "while" expr NL block
print       ::= "print" "(" ( string | expr ) ")"
string      ::= '"' { any byte except '"' and newline } '"'
block       ::= "{" NL { statement } [ exit_stmt NL ] "}" NL   (not empty)
exit_stmt   ::= "exit" factor
expr        ::= arith [ ( "==" | "!=" ) arith ]
arith       ::= term { ( "+" | "-" ) term }
term        ::= factor { "*" factor }
factor      ::= number | "true" | "false" | ident | field | chain | "!" factor
field       ::= ident "." ident { "." ident }
chain       ::= ( call | member_call ) { "." ident [ "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")" ] }
call        ::= ident "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"
member_call ::= ident { "." ident } "." ident "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"

Terminals ident and number come from the lexer as "identifier" and
"constant" tokens; how they are spelled is the lexer's rule, not the
grammar's. "struct", "fn", "i32", "i64", "bool", "mut", "exit", "true",
"false", "if", "else", "while", "print" are the lexer's keywords
("print" joined them this practice - a variable, field, parameter or
function can no longer be named print, the same way none of them could
ever be named exit or if), "{"/"}" block tokens, "("/")" their own token
kind (so a call's parentheses never read as a block), ":="/"->"/"=="/
"!="/"!"/"."/"," operator tokens, "+"/"-"/"*" single-byte operator
tokens, used here with the text they carry. "string" is its own token
kind, read byte by byte between two '"' with no escape sequences - an
unterminated one (newline or end of file before the closing '"') is a
lexer error at the opening '"'. read is not a keyword: read() parses
through the ordinary call rule like any other name, and is reserved
instead in checker.py (see below), the one place a name is otherwise free
to be anything. NL is the end of a line: one statement is one line,
except if/while/struct/fn/block, which are several - the parser keeps a
line cursor next to its token cursor for exactly this reason.

A struct's own fn (a member function) reuses the fn rule verbatim: same
":=", parameter list, "->" and result type as a top-level fn, same block
for its body. The only syntactic difference is where it is allowed -
struct's field_decl { field_decl } { fn } puts every field before every
member function: a field after a member function is a parser error at
that field, a member function before the struct's first field is a
parser error at its fn. A field and a member function of one struct (and
two member functions of one struct) cannot share a name either, but that
is a name-table check in checker.py, not a grammar rule.

A member_call stands wherever a factor can - after "exit", inside an
expr, as a decl's or an argument's value. Its object is a field chain of
any length (ident { "." ident }, same as field, but at least the
receiver itself) and the very next "." ident "(" after it is the member
function's name and call; a plain call (just ident "(" ...")", no
leading dots) is a different factor alternative, and field without a
following "(" stays a field access, not a call - the parser decides which
of field/call/member_call it is by peeking one token past each
identifier. A member function is called on an object, never on a type:
that the identifier in front names an actual variable (not just any
identifier, and never a struct's own name) is, again, checker.py's job -
the grammar does not distinguish a type name from a variable name any
more than it did a struct name from any other ident in Practice 6.

chain is the additional task: once a call or member_call has produced a
value, "." can continue past it - "." ident alone reads a field of that
value (extractvalue in gen.py: there is no variable behind a call's
result to take the address of, so this is never a gep), "." ident "("
calls a member function on it (gen.py stores the value into a fresh
alloca and passes that address as the receiver, the same as any other
member_call, just built from a temporary instead of a named object). The
parser accepts this after every call unconditionally; that the value
actually has a struct type when "." follows (a field or a further member
call needs one to make sense) is checker.py's job, not the grammar's -
the same split as a field or a call's own argument types. The chain never
reaches assign: assign's own grammar never gained parentheses, so a line
shaped like a chain followed by ":=" is caught earlier, by the same
look-ahead that already rejects a bare call as a statement (it stops at
the first "(" it finds after any number of leading ". ident", exactly
where a chain would also start) - a dedicated error was not needed.

A struct's type is a built-in type or an ident naming a struct declared
above it, or the struct's own name for one of its member function's
parameters or result type (checker.py registers a struct before checking
its own member functions' signatures for exactly this reason - a field
still cannot be of the struct's own type, that would make its size
unbounded, but a method returning or taking the type it is itself
declared on has no such problem, it only ever sees it through a pointer
or hands back a value it already built); the grammar does not distinguish
a struct type from any other ident - that a given name was actually
declared, and what it names, is semantics, not syntax (the parser
dispatches a line starting with ident ident... to decl the same way it
dispatches "i32 x..."). Likewise a field/call's ident is any identifier;
whether it resolves to a known function or a known field of the right
type is checker.py's job.

print stands only where a statement can, never where a factor can - it
has no value, so print nested inside any expr (print(1) + 2, or as the
single argument of another print) fails the same way any other keyword
would where an ident/number/"true"/"false" was expected: print simply
is not a factor alternative. Its one argument is a bare string literal,
printed as written with no further parsing, or any expr, evaluated and
printed according to its type (checker.py's job to accept any built-in
or struct type and reject nothing else - there is nothing to reject,
every type is printable). A string literal, in turn, is only ever valid
there: one appearing as a factor is a parser error, not a checker one -
string is simply not a factor alternative either.

init_value's recursion ("{" init_value { "," init_value } "}" standing
in for a single value) lets a struct field's value be written as a
brace list instead of a variable or a call, built field by field, nested
as deep as the struct nests. decl's own outer "{" ... "}" is the same
rule one level up - a decl is just an init_value with a name and a type
in front of it.

exit_stmt still takes one operand: a constant, a variable, a field
chain, a call, a member call, a chain, or "!"-prefixed one of those,
never an operation - "exit" calls parse_factor, not parse_expr. A call
(plain or member) alone, with no "exit" and no ":=" catching its result,
is not a statement; the field/call/member_call/chain alternatives of
factor cover every other place a value is needed.

Semantics the grammar cannot express live in checker.py, not here:
- a stack of scope frames, one per block, swapped for a fresh one
  holding just the parameters when checking a function or member
  function body - the body sees its own parameters and locals and every
  function of the program by name (regardless of declaration order),
  never the top-level variables; a member function's body additionally
  sees its own struct's fields by their bare name, as if each were an
  implicit "self.field" - the receiver is passed by pointer (never
  copied), so a write through a bare field name inside a member function
  is visible to the caller once the call returns, same as if it had
  written through the object's own name from outside; a parameter or
  local named like a field hides the field, exactly as an inner block's
  variable hides an outer one's;
- a bare call f(...) inside a member function looks among that struct's
  own member functions before the program's global ones - an implicit
  call on the same object, hiding a global function of the same name;
  read() is a reserved builtin ahead of both (no field, parameter, local,
  struct or program function may ever be named read, checked wherever
  any of those introduces a name), so it can never collide with either;
- a member function is mutating if it assigns to a field of its own
  object (directly, or through a chain that starts at one of its own
  fields), or calls a mutating member function on its own object or on
  one of its fields - never through an unrelated local/parameter, and
  never through a temporary produced by a chain (the additional task):
  "the result is a temporary copy ... any member function can be called
  on it, also a mutating one", with no further obligation, since nothing
  else aliases it. Member functions may call each other in a cycle, so
  this is decided only after every body in the program has been checked,
  as a fixed point over the call graph gathered while checking them: seed
  it with the ones that assign a field directly, then repeat marking the
  callers of an already-mutating function until nothing new is marked.
  Only once this is final can a call's own object chain be checked: a
  mutating call needs every link mut - the object and each field on the
  way, the same rule as an assignment - a non-mutating one needs nothing;
- a struct's fields are checked against the structs declared strictly
  above it, so a struct can never contain itself, directly or through
  another struct - this falls out of the ordering, not a separate
  occurs-check (a member function's own parameter/result types are not
  under this rule - see above);
- a decl/call/member_call's values and arguments are checked field by
  field / parameter by parameter against the type they fill, with the
  same i32-into-i64 widening rule as before, and a single value of a
  struct's own type copies the whole object instead of filling its first
  field;
- a field is constant unless its declaration says mut; "x.y.z := e"
  needs x itself mut and every field named on the way (y, z) mut - one
  missing mut anywhere on the chain rejects the whole assignment, even
  if the variable and the final field are both mut; the same rule
  applies starting from an implicit self field inside a member function,
  with the field's own mut standing in for "x itself mut"; replacing a
  whole object with ":=" additionally needs every field of its type, at
  every depth, to be mut;
- inside a function or member function, exit's value must match the
  declared result type (with the same widening rule); at the top level
  it still has no target type to match, only the rule that it must be
  built-in, never a struct.
That a call's or member call's arguments are evaluated left to right,
and that an inner "{}" is built depth-first before the object that holds
it, is not semantics either: it falls out of a single recursive-descent
walk, the same way operator precedence did.
